From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Refuse a date without a digit before trying every form

Date.parse and new Date(string) read ECMA's own format first, then try
Qt's text, ISO and RFC 2822 forms and forty more, in turn. Each form
with a time builds a local QDateTime for its default day before reading
anything, through mktime, so a string none of them reads took about
2 ms, against under a microsecond for one ECMA's format reads. The
shell parses an absent time as an empty string in a sort's comparison,
twice for each. Every form says a year, so a string with no digit now
gives NaN at once, as it did after trying them all.

---
--- a/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4dateobject.cpp
+++ b/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4dateobject.cpp
@@ -471,6 +471,21 @@
         return TimeClip(t);
     }
 
+    // Every form read below says a year, so a string without a digit is
+    // none of them. Each form with a time builds a local QDateTime for
+    // its default day before it reads anything, so trying them all took
+    // about 2 ms for an empty string, against under a microsecond for one
+    // the format above reads.
+    bool hasDigit = false;
+    for (const QChar c : s) {
+        if (c.isDigit()) {
+            hasDigit = true;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+    if (!hasDigit)
+        return qt_qnan();
+
     QDateTime dt = QDateTime::fromString(s, Qt::TextDate);
     if (!dt.isValid())
         dt = QDateTime::fromString(s, Qt::ISODate);
